/* Localizable.strings — master English bundle for OpenMatch iOS.
 *
 * This is the source of truth for every user-facing string the app
 * renders outside the error registry. Backend error copy lives in
 * ErrorMessages.strings (mirrors `backend/src/lib/error-codes.ts`).
 *
 * Naming: <feature>.<section>.<element>
 *   - feature: onboarding | welcome | swipe | match | profile | chat |
 *              likes | settings | common
 *   - section: optional — empty_state, action_row, toolbar, alert, etc.
 *   - element: title | subtitle | body | button | label | hint | a11y_label
 *
 * Format specifiers:
 *   - %@   string (e.g. display name)
 *   - %lld integer (e.g. age, count)
 *   - %.1f one-decimal float (e.g. distance in km)
 *   - %1$@ / %2$@ positional when order varies per language
 *
 * Translation workflow:
 *   1. Copy this file to <lang>.lproj/Localizable.strings.
 *   2. Replace each `"value"` with the translated string, preserving
 *      all `%@` / `%lld` / `%.1f` specifiers and their order via the
 *      positional `%1$@` form when the target language reorders them.
 *   3. Run `genstrings` against the SwiftUI sources to verify no new
 *      keys are missing.
 *   4. Open a PR; CI flags any stub `???key???` value still in the
 *      shipped locale bundle.
 *
 * Stub locales (es / fr / de / ja) intentionally carry `???key???` as
 * their value so a contributor can locate what still needs translation.
 * We do NOT ship machine-translations.
 */

